# OS: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS | 2026-10-26T09:18:16Z | validator-host
$ bash logship/pft-logship.sh plan
pft-logship plan - 2026-10-26T09:18:16Z - Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS
peer: <unset: PEER_NAME in /etc/pft-logship.env> | peer address: <unset>
systemd-journal-remote package: not installed

== /etc/systemd/journal-upload.conf.d/pft-logship.conf
# pft-logship: upload this host's journal to <peer> over mutual TLS
[Upload]
URL=https://<peer>:19532
ServerKeyFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/upload.key
ServerCertificateFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/upload.crt
TrustedCertificateFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/ca.crt

== /etc/systemd/journal-remote.conf.d/pft-logship.conf
# pft-logship: receive the peer's journal; clients must present a cert from the pft-logship CA
[Remote]
Seal=false
SplitMode=host
ServerKeyFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/remote.key
ServerCertificateFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/remote.crt
TrustedCertificateFile=/etc/pft-logship/tls/ca.crt

== firewall
ufw allow proto tcp from <peer> to any port 19532 comment pft-logship

== steps apply will take
1. apt-get install systemd-journal-remote (receiver + uploader units, disabled until configured)
2. /etc/pft-logship/tls: this host makes its own key + CSR (keys never leave the host); the CA on the validator signs both CSRs
   (CSR and certificate travel over ssh, keys do not); 0640 root:systemd-journal-remote for remote.key
3. write the two drop-ins above + /etc/systemd/system/systemd-journal-upload.service.d/pft-logship.conf (LoadCredential for upload.key: journal-upload runs as a DynamicUser)
4. UFW rule above (peer address only), then enable --now systemd-journal-remote.socket and systemd-journal-upload
5. storage cap 2G per sender: pft-logship-vacuum.timer runs journalctl --directory=/var/log/journal/remote
   --vacuum-size (journal-remote.conf in systemd 249 has no MaxUse=)
6. pft-integrity accept for the new package/units/config (no postfiatd restart at any step)
exit 0
